"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="feather feather-chevron-down" style="fill: none;stroke: currentColor;width: var(--rsw, 100%);height: 100%" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R17dau:" aria-controls="replo-:R17dau:" aria-expanded="false"><div data-rid="3de7041e-21ce-4d10-8ef4-9f2003de5642" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-tsqlvg"><div data-rid="512d199a-d1c2-4e19-b980-c03212375dc8" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-1apgepa alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>As men get older, it's common for hormone levels, including testosterone, to gradually decrease. Maintaining healthy testosterone levels is essential for various aspects of well-being, including sexual health, cognitive function, weight management, mood regulation, and metabolic support. This product features several potent ingredients, one being the patented ingredient Tesnor®, a unique blend of pomegranate peel and cocoa seed extracts, which has been shown to help support both total and free testosterone levels.<br></p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="a11beeea-9b56-4128-906d-8b8aa5169c96" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-7byucr" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed" data-orientation="vertical"><div data-rid="88e7751c-d0c2-471a-8197-ec0c960c0207" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="replo-:R1fdau:" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1cw16c1"><div data-rid="39a243a7-722e-4207-86bc-11e173cb488a" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-1b6822d alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>What is Free Testosterone?</p></span></div><div data-rid="16b4c8d8-8285-4ec1-8078-fcc9fff57145" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-15dz5qs"><span style="display:contents"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="feather feather-chevron-down" style="fill: none;stroke: currentColor;width: var(--rsw, 100%);height: 100%" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R1fdau:" aria-controls="replo-:R1fdau:" aria-expanded="false"><div data-rid="3416bb4e-209c-44b7-a134-42ca7f1e93bb" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-18w4yj9"><div data-rid="c7f2ab18-5dfa-4a61-aaf7-8a7741300959" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0.0" class="r-idfxs6 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Free testosterone is the portion of testosterone in the bloodstream that is not bound to proteins, such as sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) or albumin. Unlike bound testosterone, which is attached to these proteins, free testosterone is biologically active and readily available for the body to use. It plays a crucial role in various physiological functions, including muscle growth, sexual function, mood regulation, energy levels, and overall well-being.</p><p>Since free testosterone is the active form, its levels are often a key indicator of how effectively testosterone is functioning in the body, even if total testosterone levels appear normal. Monitoring free testosterone is important, especially for assessing hormonal health in men as they age.</p></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="399302d7-b735-4726-a0fc-6cf0dfa587ef" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0" class="r-1qcyisc"><div class="r-1qksuu7" data-rid="1"><div data-rid="b032f494-0e86-464a-b358-0f7854b119ab" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0" class="r-ndjfzd"><button data-rid="a0db3b11-9660-415c-86f6-d5d205e4de2e" data-replo-repeated-index=".0.0.0.0.0" tabindex="0" role="li